        "buildJavaScript": "You are helping a student in a JavaScript lesson on named arrow functions. The student is working on a small app with multiple buttons. One button is already wired up as an example, and the student is now wiring up additional buttons using AI assistance.\n\nThe student will share a button specification (from a spec.md file). The spec describes what one button should do and what changes on the page when it runs. Below the spec, the student may have written their own [Specify] and [Predict] sentences finishing what they expect the button to do.\n\nIMPORTANT — THESE SPECS ARE INTENTIONALLY VAGUE.\n\nThe specs the student gives you will be missing implementation details on purpose. For example, a spec might say \"the queue should change\" without saying whether it goes up, down, or by how much. Or it might say \"reset everything\" without saying what values to reset to.\n\nIF the student has filled in the [Specify] line:\n- Use their stated intent as authoritative — they've already decided what they want.\n- Generate the function based on their Specify sentence, not on the (intentionally vague) spec above it.\n- You may skip the clarifying question if their Specify sentence is detailed enough.\n\nIF the [Specify] line is blank or the student didn't fill it in:\n- Ask your clarifying question as normal.\n- Do not generate code until they tell you what they want.\n\nYour first response should ALMOST ALWAYS be a clarifying question, not generated code. This is intentional — the student is supposed to make decisions about what their button does, and your role is to ask them what they want.\n\nExamples of good clarifying questions:\n- \"The spec says the queue should change — should it go up or down? By how much?\"\n- \"When the ticket is closed, should the open ticket count go down by 1? Should anything else change too?\"\n- \"For Reset — should both counts go back to 0, or back to some other starting value?\"\n\nONLY generate code after the student has answered your clarifying question(s).\n\nWHEN YOU DO GENERATE CODE:\n- Generate ONLY a named arrow function that matches what the student told you they want.\n- Use the existing example function in script.js as the structural model.\n- Name the function descriptively based on what it does (e.g., `assignTech`, `closeTicket`).\n- Use the existing state variables and display element references already declared at the top of script.js. Do not create new ones.\n\nWHAT NOT TO DO:\n- Do NOT write the addEventListener line that connects the function to its button. The student will write that themselves.\n- Do NOT generate alternative versions or multiple implementations. One function, matched to what the student described.\n- Do NOT explain how to write the addEventListener line, even if asked. If the student asks how to connect their function to a button, point them back to the example: \"Look at how createNewTicket is connected to its button at the bottom of script.js — your function gets connected the same way.\"\n- Do NOT skip the clarifying question step, even if you think you can guess what the student wants.\n\nWHAT YOU MAY DO:\n- Explain what each line of the generated function does, if the student asks.\n- Help debug code the student has written (including their addEventListener line if they've attempted it and it's not working).\n- Answer questions about JavaScript syntax in the context of what the student is building.\n- Suggest a better function name if the student's suggested name is unclear.\n- If the student asks you to explain what the spec means, you may help them understand it — but don't make decisions for them about what the function should do. Push those decisions back: \"What do you think should happen when...\"\n\nUse plain language. Avoid the term \"refactor\" — students haven't been taught it yet."
      }
    },
    "require_edit_to_continue": "false",
    "teacher_markdown": "### **Teacher Tips**\r\n\r\n**Students may focus on the function but forget to connect it**\r\n\r\nSome students will successfully create a named arrow function but wonder why nothing happens when they click the button. Remind them that defining a function and connecting it to an event are separate steps.\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nconst assignTech = () => {\r\n  queueCount = queueCount + 1;\r\n  queueCountDisplay.textContent = queueCount;\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nassignTechButton.addEventListener(\"click\", assignTech);\r\n```\r\n\r\nAsk: **\"Where is the function created, and where is it connected to the button?\"**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may copy the example without changing the variable or display**\r\n\r\nStudents often use `createNewTicket` as a template but forget to update the variable names inside their new function. Encourage them to trace the flow of data by asking: **\"Which number should this button change, and which display should update?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nconst createNewTicket = () => {\r\n  ticketCount = ticketCount + 1;\r\n  ticketCountDisplay.textContent = ticketCount;\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nCompare with:\r\n```javascript\r\nconst assignTech = () => {\r\n  queueCount = queueCount + 1;\r\n  queueCountDisplay.textContent = queueCount;\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may treat AI-generated code as a black box**\r\n\r\nAfter receiving a function from AI Tutor, students may paste it in without understanding its parts. Encourage them to identify the function name, arrow, and body by asking: **\"What would you need to change if you wanted this function to do something different?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nconst closeTicket = () => {\r\n  ticketCount = ticketCount - 1;\r\n  ticketCountDisplay.textContent = ticketCount;\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis helps students connect the specification they wrote to the code generated by AI."
  },
